defmodule Solution do
  @doc """
  Determines whether a given integer is a power of two.

  A power of two is a positive integer that can be expressed as 2^k
  for some non-negative integer k.

  0 and negative integers are not powers of two.

  This implementation runs in constant time using bitwise operations.
  """
  @spec check_power_of_two(integer()) :: boolean()
  def check_power_of_two(n) when not is_integer(n) do
    false
  end

  def check_power_of_two(n) when n <= 0 do
    false
  end

  def check_power_of_two(n) do
    Bitwise.band(n, n - 1) == 0
  end
end
===AFTER===
defmodule Solution do
  @doc """
  Determines whether a given integer is a power of two.

  A power of two is a positive integer that can be expressed as 2^k
  for some non-negative integer k.

  0 and negative integers are not powers of two.

  This implementation runs in constant time using bitwise operations.
  """
  @spec check_power_of_two(integer()) :: boolean()
  def check_power_of_two(n) when n <= 0 do
    false
  end

  def check_power_of_two(n) do
    Bitwise.band(n, n - 1) == 0
  end
end
===ASSUMPTIONS===
===RATIONALE===
A function clause whose guard is solely a negated type-check (e.g. `when not is_integer(n)`) silently swallows contract violations that the @spec already rules out. Removing the clause lets Elixir crash on bad input per "let it crash", while preserving identical behaviour for all spec-conformant inputs. This is a common anti-pattern in LLM-generated code.
